        Fact("anagram", "language", "An anagram rearranges the letters of another word or phrase.", "Each letter must be used with the same multiplicity. Merely using some of the letters is a different kind of puzzle.", "‘Listen’ and ‘silent’ use exactly the same letters.", "Merriam-Webster — anagram: https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/anagram"),
        Fact("homophone", "language", "Homophones sound alike but can differ in spelling and meaning.", "The relation depends on pronunciation, so accent can affect which words count as homophones.", "‘Sea’ and ‘see’ are a familiar English pair.", "Merriam-Webster — homophone: https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/homophone"),
        Fact("etymology", "language", "Etymology studies the history and origins of words.", "A word's historical origin does not necessarily determine its current meaning. Meanings change through use.", "A modern dictionary definition and a word's origin answer different questions.", "Merriam-Webster — etymology: https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/etymology"),
        Fact("mercury", "space", "Mercury is the closest planet to the Sun, but Venus is hotter on average.", "Venus has a thick atmosphere with a powerful greenhouse effect. Distance from a star is not the only influence on temperature.", "A planet’s atmosphere can strongly change the surface conditions expected from distance alone.", "NASA Science — Mercury facts: https://science.nasa.gov/mercury/facts/"),
        Fact("jupiter", "space", "Jupiter is the largest planet in our Solar System.", "It is a gas giant whose diameter is about eleven times Earth’s. Diameter and mass are different comparisons.", "Eleven Earth diameters span roughly one Jupiter diameter; that does not mean only eleven Earth volumes fit inside it.", "NASA Science — Jupiter facts: https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/facts/"),
        Fact("saturn", "space", "Saturn’s rings contain countless pieces of ice and rock.", "They are not one solid disc. Many individual particles orbit Saturn.", "A distant smooth-looking band can consist of many separate objects.", "NASA Science — Saturn facts: https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/facts/"),
